Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com> wrote that he can see how axioms
might be used to provide a foundation for logic and math, which he would
also find meaningful, but he didn't see how this method could be applied
in ethics.  I think the way this can be done is through praxaeology, the
science of human action, since human conduct (ethics) is a subcategory of
praxaeology (I'm following Rothbard's classification of social and human
sciences in "Man, Economy, and State" on this).

>As for the second part, I can't see how one can derive a system of natural
>rights from mathematics, and I can't see any axioms that can be defended
>by asserting the denial presupposes leading to anything that says more
>than mathematics does about the world.

I wouldn't take such a monist position that natural rights can be derived
from foundational axioms - just as I wouldn't say that economics can be,
either.  Rather, I'd say that conclusions derived from foundational axioms
are necessary conditions to the theory of natural rights, but not sufficient
ones unto themselves, again, just as in economics.  We also need to make
some empirical assumptions in order to complete the theory.

>I know I'm not being precise, but it's difficult for me to come up with
>suggestions about how you can convince me of something that I find so
>implausible. 

Just indicating what you find implausible about it is a good place to start.

>And finally, yes, I have read most of HUMAN ACTION,

Wow.  I'm impressed - I still haven't managed to do that.  I keep getting
lost in his German syntax.  I've found that the only way I can maintain
attention to both his and Hayek's work is to read it aloud doing my best
imitiation (pretty bad) of a German accent.  Then it all clicks.  Otherwise,
I get lost and my eyes glaze over.  You're at the advanced level - I'm still
at the intermediate, having only read Rothbard's Man, Economy, and State.

