From: carlf@media.mit.edu (Carl Feynman)
Date: Tue, 1 Mar 1994 01:56:23 -0100
Subject: [#94-3-3] Water Fuel Cells

Geoff Burch writes:
>        I have a question pertaining to Water Fuel Cell technology.  I
>recently met a man named Stan Meyer who was a customer in a cafe where I
>work, who claims to have designed a way to convert all heat-engines to
>engines that run off of the covalent bonds between hydrogen and oxygen,
>thus creating a non-pollutive, efficient machine that the fuel will never
>run out for.  Steve, upon request furnished an inch-thick brochure
>containing the information based upon how it works.
>
>        Has anyone heard of this?  What info can someone please send me
>concerning this?  What is the validity and possibility of it's fruition?

This is a con.  The law of conservation of energy (also known as the first
law of thermodynamics) says you can't do this, and that's one of the
best-established physical laws.  

The first machine to run endlessly off water that I'm aware of was
"invented" by a man named Keeley about a century ago.  He got away with
more than a million bucks before his investors caught on.  Periodically
someone tries something like this again.  

I remember seeing a prospectus for a similar perpetual motion machine in
the '70s; it was only half an inch thick, so those con artists didn't do as
much writing as your Mr. Meyer.  But they did build a sustantial portion of
the device, which worked.  They told people they needed investment to
finish the design.  By carefully analyzing their plans, you could find that
the violation of physical law was in the not-yet-built portion.  It worked
by violating the second law of thermodynamics.  There was a heat exchanger
that took in two streams of lukewarm freon and discharged a cold stream and
a hot stream.

If this guy is asking folks for money, you might want to report him to your
local bunco squad.  If he's not asking for money, he's just a harmless nut.

Welcome to the list, Geoff!


