Eric Forste wrote: <Thus the truth that we can hold in our heads and use is
necessarily imperfect, and only asymptotically approaches the mystical One Truth
that you like to talk about, and that I acknowledge does exist, but is not of
much practical use to us.>

I understand what you mean by "mystical One Truth" and I acknowledge it exists
also.  However, I did not capitalize it, and I wasn't really meaning it in some
sort of universal scale.  I was thinking more in terms of human scales of truth,
accessing as closely as we can, the truth that occurs in our lives.  Implicit in
this is the Aristotelian position of no logical contradictions.  This is more
what I meant when I said that "there is only one truth," that we should not hold
two opposites to be true simultaneously.

Eric again: <Truth may not be relative, but certainty is.>

My problem with this formulation is that my dictionary definition of (and my
historical usage of the word) "certain" and "certainty" refers to "indisputible"
and "beyond doubt."  With this understanding of the word in my mind, I cannot
help but think that what you are talking about is more what I would term
"belief" rather than "certainty."  E.g., "Truth may not be relative, but
*belief* is."  My brain does not assimilate your statement on "relative
certainty" no matter how it is rearranged, my inoculation against leftist
nihilism kicks in.

Eric again: <Why do you believe it makes sense to say that "You cannot access
reality infallibly" is an infallible statement of reality?> and <I am a realist
materialist. I just don't think that knowledge comes easy, nor that human
knowledge can ever be anything other than an increasingly-better *approximation*
of the truth.>

I too would characterize myself as a qualified realist materialist.  The
materialist part means I believe that existence is concrete and particulate,
i.e., indivisible, uncreatable, indestructible spacetime particles.  In answer
to your "How can existence not be existence?" I would say when QM physicists and
cosmologists postulate that the quantum vacuum can exist without spacetime
particles.  They can postulate it within mathematics but it does not reflect
objective reality.  I hold to the ontological interpretation of QM of the
"undivided universe" (supported by evidence) in conjunction with particulate
existence.  Without spacetime particles, there would be no existence, without
the quantum vacuum surrounding them, there would be no movement.  The quantum
vacuum is caused by the spacetime particles.  The spacetime particles are not
caused, this is why I hold "existence exists" as an axiom.  You have to have a
starting place, some ground to stand on or you can't discuss much of anything
because meaning becomes amorphous.  When Big Bang cosmologies go off into
quantum fuzz, one-ended tunnels, singularities and creation ex nihilo, they are
not describing existence.

The "realist" part is like James Robert Brown describes it in his new book
"Smoke and Mirrors:  How Science Reflects Reality" Routledge (1993).  Truth (the
facts of objective reality) exists independently from us; we can make rational
(though fallible) choices among rival theories; and science aims at the truth.
Because knowledge resides in consensual reality, and consensual reality is a
different aspect than objective reality, fallibility is inherent in consensual
reality.  Knowledge, mathematics, logic all contain value (discrimination
between good and bad) ultimately derived from first-person subjective purpose.

The "qualified" refers to your later reference to approximations of truth.
There are unbroachable epistemological walls surrounding consensual reality
preventing us from direct access to objective or subjective reality, such as the
uncertainty principle, the dynamical speed of complex biological systems such as
the genomic regulatory system or neurological processes and the limits of the
"observable" universe.

Eric again: <And it is knowledge that PCR is interested in explaining, not the
One Truth.>

I really am confused.  Here again PCR is described as epistemology (theory of
knowledge) rather than a prescription for rationality (the Socratic method).  It
seems to alternate between these two.

Mike Price wrote: <Since the evidence, like eg the cosmic microwave background
temperature of 4K and the observed ratios of light isotopes, supports the Big
Bang
(or some close inflationary relative) then it must be your ideas on
causality that are defective.>

Given my certainty in the particulate nature of existence and the "undivided
universe" interpretation of QM, my understanding of causality (i.e., everything
is caused by something else without exception) does not permit me to accept
creation ex nihilo.  The ratios of light isotopes are a sticky point in Big Bang
cosmologies and both this evidence and the background radiation have alternate
theories based on historical evolutionary processes explaining them.

